<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Tajik ASSR</h1><p class="st-meta">1924 CE – 1929 CE · capital Dushanbe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Tajik national republic, created in 1924 as an autonomous part of Soviet Uzbekistan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the national delimitation of 1924 the mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a>-speaking mountain districts of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> were organised as the Tajik ASSR within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-ssr">Uzbek SSR</a>. The village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> was chosen as capital and grew rapidly.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was a poor, mountainous territory still fighting the last Basmachi bands. Soviet authorities built roads, schools and irrigation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and promoted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajik</a> language, which was written in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet from 1928. Tajik intellectuals argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, both Tajik-speaking cities, had been wrongly given to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1929 the republic was separated from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> and raised to a union republic, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tajik-ssr">Tajik SSR</a>, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khujand">Khujand</a> region added. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> was renamed Stalinabad. Disputes over the borders drawn in 1924 continued to colour relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tajik-assr.jpg" alt="Tajik ASSR" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Transoxsiana · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tajik_Autonomous_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1929%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1924 CE – 1929 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dushanbe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nusratullo Maksum, Abdurahim Hojibayev</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tajik</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tajik Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1926&amp;focus=tajik-assr" aria-label="Tajik ASSR on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6388 793 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tajik ASSR lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6708 1049L6691 960M6708 1049L6708 1050"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6691 960h0M6708 1050h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6708 1049h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6708 1049h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dushanbe. <a href="/atlas?year=1926&amp;focus=tajik-assr">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
